    "question_id": "q-hyperbola-pair",
    "task_fidelity": {
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The paired complete-graph descriptions retain hyperbola type, both signed branches, exclusion of both axes, both axis asymptotes and actual pairs satisfying the original equations. A and D have identical quadrant/asymptote statements and the same x coordinates, but their y coordinates differ by a factor of ten. Sign/category knowledge cannot distinguish A from D: coordinate substitution or the product scale is necessary. Replacing 6,-12 by 60,-120 makes the complete correct set {D}, not {A}. This therefore tests the original quantitative scale in addition to qualitative shape. The internal limitations explicitly disclaim independently selecting enough samples, plotting points and continuously drawing both branches; source coverage also disclaims drawing the asymptotic trend. This is an honestly limited recognition task, not evidence of completed actual graph construction. Reasonable elimination is not counted as an automatic failure."
    },
    "redundancy": {
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "Within this changed item, B tests reversed product signs, C tests unjustified restriction to positive x, and D tests an incorrect factor-ten coordinate scale. A and D deliberately share type, quadrants and asymptotes but are not equivalent: their pairs have products 6/-12 versus 60/-120. Eliminating B and C by signs/domain still leaves a numerical relationship to check between A and D. No extra response unit or question was added. The other 27 questions and their already passed package relationships are inherited, not reopened or independently re-established here."
    },
    "answer_leakage": {
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The visible stem gives only the two original equations and requests their complete graphs. It exposes no source number, correct set, rationale or mapping. A, B and D use parallel two-branch/asymptote descriptions with the same number of coordinate pairs; A and D cannot be distinguished by their identical qualitative wording. Candidate coordinates must be tested against the equations and are not copied solved-table entries or a visible answer key. No other question, source answer or prior choice key was read or used to determine A; prior package-level PASS evidence is inherited within the authorized unchanged scope."
    },
    "mathematics": {
      "verdict": "pass",
      "independent_solution": "Before internal disclosure, I obtained both full solution sets {(x,6/x): x is real and x!=0} and {(x,-12/x): x is real and x!=0}. Their ranges also exclude zero. Both have two branches and asymptotes x=0,y=0, with no points on either axis. The first has xy=6 in quadrants I/III and contains (2,3),(-2,-3); the second has xy=-12 in quadrants II/IV and contains (3,-4),(-3,4). Only A states all these facts. B instead uses products -6,+12, C omits legal negative-x branches, and D uses products +60,-120 despite correct qualitative geometry. The complete independent true set {A} and all four sealed truths agree with the subsequently disclosed author key and rationales.",
      "correct": ["A"],
      "option_checks": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "truth": "correct",
          "evidence": "2*3=(-2)*(-3)=6 and 3*(-4)=(-3)*4=-12. Positive/negative x supply both correctly stated branches. Nonzero products forbid axis points, and the reciprocal limits give both axis asymptotes. The internal derivation and its null misconception agree with this complete calculation."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "evidence": "The pairs give 2*(-3)=(-2)*3=-6 and 3*4=(-3)*(-4)=12, opposite to the required products. The disclosed wrong sign procedure really gives y=-6/x and y=12/x: x=2,-2 yields y=-3,3 and x=3,-3 yields y=4,-4, exactly the listed points and reversed quadrants. The shared true asymptote statements do not rescue the false conjunction."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "evidence": "(2,3) and (3,-4) do satisfy the two originals. But (-2,-3) and (-3,4) do too, supplying the omitted third/second-quadrant branches. The disclosed wrong procedure strengthens x!=0 to x>0, which indeed retains only the positive-x points and one branch of each graph. This is a real domain-loss error rather than wrong sample arithmetic."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "evidence": "2*30=(-2)*(-30)=60 and 3*(-40)=(-3)*40=-120, so none of the listed points has the original product. The internal sampling/label mismatch is exact: evaluating 6/(x/10)=60/x at labelled x=2,-2 gives y=30,-30, and evaluating -12/(x/10)=-120/x at labelled x=3,-3 gives y=-40,40. Plotting these results at x rather than x/10 preserves quadrants and asymptotes while producing the listed factor-ten scale error."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "source_review": {
    "source_number": "94",
    "independent_solution": "Source 94 asks to construct the graphs of (a) y=6/x and (b) xy=-12. Definitions 11 and 14 make a graph the set of every real coordinate pair satisfying its equation; definition 15 classifies xy=k with k!=0 as a hyperbola. For (a), multiplying by its nonzero x gives xy=6, and the full graph is {(x,6/x): x is real, x!=0}. It contains (2,3),(-2,-3), has one branch for all x>0 in quadrant I and another for all x<0 in quadrant III. For (b), a nonzero product excludes both x=0 and y=0 and gives the full graph {(x,-12/x): x is real, x!=0}. It contains (3,-4),(-3,4), with branches in quadrants IV and II respectively. For both, |x| increasing without bound sends y towards zero without reaching it; x tending to zero from either side sends |y| to infinity. Thus x=0 and y=0 are asymptotes, neither axis has graph points, and both signed branches belong to each complete graph. The coordinate products must be 6 and -12, not merely positive and negative: (2,30) and (3,-40), for example, describe products 60 and -120. This solution was derived from the allowed seed and definitions before reading the view or internal rationale; no approved old solution was reused.",
    "coverage": {
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The new source 94 coverage points to q-hyperbola-pair, whose source_numbers is exactly [94]. Its paired response retains both original subparts and tests their full two-branch geometry, nonzero domain, axis/asymptote distinction and constants' coordinate scale. A/D share all qualitative claims yet have products 6/-12 versus 60/-120; both signed coordinate pairs in A match the originals, and the counterfactual constants 60/-120 change the unique answer to D. Thus the former sign-only source-94 loss has been repaired rather than silently omitted. Coverage.omitted_tasks explicitly records that autonomous sample choice/plotting, continuous drawing of both branches and presenting the asymptotic trend are not tested, consistent with intent.limitations and the absence of figures. Textual recognition and reasonable elimination do not automatically fail this gate, but neither is represented as completed drawing. The other 13 approved source reviews and all unrelated passed stages are inherited without rereading or rerunning; this is source 94 approval only, not whole-lesson approval."
    }
  },
